---
layout: base.njk
bodyClass: docs-home-page
---
{% from "install.njk" import packageManagers %}
{#- The specimens below are declared once and used twice: rendered live, and
    highlighted into the source pane beside them. One string, so the markup a
    reader copies is the markup that produced what they are looking at: the
    drift this page has had before (a snippet that declared attributes the
    rendered output no longer had) cannot happen when there is only one copy.

    They are written flush to the left margin on purpose: whitespace inside a
    `set` block is captured verbatim, and this is the indentation that ends up
    in the <pre>. -#}

{#- 1. Article + table. Structure, and the two axes of a real table
    header. Rows on one line, which is how table rows are actually written
    and what keeps the listing to a screenful: at one element per line this
    was 27 lines against the next example's 14, so switching tabs moved the
    page 240px and the "short" claim was made by a long listing. -#}
{%- set articleSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Team</h3></header>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Ada Lovelace</th><td>Engineer</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Grace Hopper</th><td>Designer</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <footer><button type="button">Invite</button></footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- 2. Disclosure. `name` on a <details> is what makes the group
    exclusive, and it is the browser doing it: the same four elements
    render the questions under "Before you install" at the bottom of this
    page. A plausible little interface rather than a specimen sheet: the
    delivery options of a checkout. -#}
{%- set detailsSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Delivery</h3></header>

  <details name="delivery" open>
    <summary>Standard delivery</summary>
    <p>Three to five working days, tracked. Free.</p>
  </details>
  <details name="delivery">
    <summary>Express delivery</summary>
    <p>Next working day if ordered before 14:00.</p>
  </details>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- 3. Form controls + validation. `type="email"` and `required` are the
    whole validation layer: the browser decides when the field is wrong and
    `:user-invalid` decides when to say so, which is after the reader has
    caused it rather than on load.

    No <form> element and no submit button, which is a deliberate limit on
    the sample rather than an omission from it. A <form> with no action
    submits to this page, so a reader who filled the field in and pressed
    Save would be navigated off the home page mid-demo, and the only ways
    to stop that are an inline handler or a script, in the section whose
    whole claim is that there is neither. Constraint validation does not
    need a form owner: `required` and `type="email"` still make the control
    invalid, and `:user-invalid` still waits for the reader to cause it,
    which is the part Cirth paints and the part worth showing. -#}
{%- set formSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Notifications</h3></header>

  <label>
    Email
    <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="you@example.com" required>
  </label>

  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" name="notes" role="switch" checked>
    Send release notes
  </label>

  <button type="button">Save</button>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- The interface the theme showcase rethemes. Not one of the three above:
    that section's subject is the stylesheet, so the sample has to hold the
    surfaces a theme actually moves: a card edge, a control border, a
    filled button, an accent. -#}
{%- set themeSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header>
    <h3>Account settings</h3>
  </header>

  <label>
    Display name
    <input name="name" value="Ada Lovelace">
  </label>

  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" role="switch" checked>
    Email notifications
  </label>

  <footer>
    <button type="button">Save changes</button>
    <button type="button" class="secondary outline">Cancel</button>
  </footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- One definition for the three examples, so the tab strip, the panels and
    the source panes cannot disagree about how many there are or what they
    are called. `note` is the sentence under each example: it is where the
    behaviour, the accessibility and the dogfooding claims land now that
    they are properties of a sample rather than sections of their own. -#}
{%- set examples = [
  {
    id: "article",
    tab: "Article",
    name: "Article + table",
    markup: articleSpecimen,
    note: "Nine elements, no classes. <code>&lt;th scope&gt;</code> on both axes is what gives a screen reader real row and column navigation. An ARIA re-implementation of a table cannot be reached this way."
  },
  {
    id: "details",
    tab: "Details",
    name: "Disclosure group",
    markup: detailsSpecimen,
    note: "Open one and the other closes: <code>name</code> makes the group exclusive and the browser runs it. The questions under <a href=\"#faq-title\">Before you install</a> are this same element, once per question."
  },
  {
    id: "form",
    tab: "Form",
    name: "Form + validation",
    markup: formSpecimen,
    note: "Type an address that is not one, then leave the field. <code>required</code> and <code>type=\"email\"</code> are the whole validation layer, and <code>:user-invalid</code> holds the error styling back until the reader has caused it, so nothing is painted red on arrival."
  }
] -%}

{#- What kind of claim each proof cell is making. One vocabulary for the
    strip and the list below it, defined once so the two cannot drift into
    saying the same thing with different words.

    · guarantee: a property the project intends to keep; a break is a bug.
    · capability: something Cirth lets you do. It says nothing about what
      your application does with it.
    · current fact: true of this build, measured, and free to move.

    The kind is a word and nothing else: a glyph beside it said the same
    thing a second time, in a register the page does not otherwise use. -#}
{%- macro proofKind(kind) -%}
{%- if kind == "guarantee" -%}
<span class="docs-proof-state">Guarantee</span>
{%- elif kind == "capability" -%}
<span class="docs-proof-state">Capability</span>
{%- else -%}
<span class="docs-proof-state">Current fact</span>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The FAQ's disclosure, declared here rather than inline in the loop that
    renders it, because the Details example above claims that this list is
    the same element it is showing. One definition keeps that claim
    checkable from one place.

    The answers arrive already substituted (see the loop): two of them
    quote a fact the build measures (the Browserslist target and the size
    of this build), and front matter is data, not a template. A marker keeps
    the answer readable in index.md and un-substitutable into nonsense: an
    HTML comment reads as nothing at all if one ever survives. -#}
{%- macro faqDisclosure(question, answer) -%}
<details name="faq">
  <summary>{{ question }}</summary>
  <p>{{ answer | safe }}</p>
</details>
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The hero's specimen: the source pane and the rendered card beside it
    are this one string, so the markup a reader copies is the markup that
    produced the card. Written to fit a phone's code pane without a
    horizontal scrollbar: one attribute set per line, nothing optional. -#}
{%- set heroSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <h2>Sign in</h2>
  <form>
    <label>
      Email
      <input type="email" required>
    </label>
    <label>
      Password
      <input type="password" required>
    </label>
    <label>
      <input type="checkbox" checked>
      Remember me
    </label>
    <button>Sign in</button>
  </form>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- The scroll story's two listings: the same card written with a class
    vocabulary, then as semantic HTML. The first is deliberately ordinary:
    the classes are the kind any utility or component vocabulary asks for,
    and the count beside it is taken from the string, not asserted. The
    second is what the third beat renders. -#}
{%- set storySoup -%}
<div class="rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-sm
            dark:border-neutral-800 dark:bg-neutral-900">
  <div class="border-b border-neutral-200 px-6 py-4
              dark:border-neutral-800">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight
               text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">
      Invite a teammate
    </h3>
  </div>
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 px-6 py-5">
    <label class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium
                  text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
      Email
      <input type="email" required class="h-10 w-full rounded-md
             border border-neutral-300 bg-transparent px-3
             text-sm focus-visible:outline-none
             focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-orange-600
             dark:border-neutral-700">
    </label>
    <label class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium
                  text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
      Role
      <select class="h-10 w-full appearance-none rounded-md
              border border-neutral-300 bg-transparent px-3
              text-sm dark:border-neutral-700">
        <option>Editor</option>
        <option>Viewer</option>
      </select>
    </label>
  </div>
  <div class="flex justify-end border-t border-neutral-200
              px-6 py-4 dark:border-neutral-800">
    <button class="inline-flex h-10 items-center rounded-md
                   bg-orange-700 px-4 text-sm font-medium
                   text-white hover:bg-orange-800">
      Send invite
    </button>
  </div>
</div>
{%- endset -%}

{%- set storySemantic -%}
<article>
  <header>
    <h3>Invite a teammate</h3>
  </header>
  <label>
    Email
    <input type="email" required>
  </label>
  <label>
    Role
    <select>
      <option>Editor</option>
      <option>Viewer</option>
    </select>
  </label>
  <footer>
    <button>Send invite</button>
  </footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- Four syntaxes that put the same element in the DOM. -#}
{%- set syntaxes = [
  { name: "HTML", code: '<button type="button">Save</button>' },
  { name: "React", code: '<button onClick={save}>Save</button>' },
  { name: "Vue", code: '<button @click="save">Save</button>' },
  { name: "Svelte", code: '<button onclick={save}>Save</button>' }
] -%}

{#- Where each ecosystem's guide lives. Names only, as text: no logo is
    drawn, and the note under the list says why they are here. -#}
{%- set ecosystems = [
  { name: "HTML", link: "/installation/cdn" },
  { name: "React", link: "/installation/react" },
  { name: "Next.js", link: "/installation/nextjs" },
  { name: "Vue", link: "/installation/vue" },
  { name: "Nuxt", link: "/installation/vue" },
  { name: "Svelte", link: "/installation/sveltekit" },
  { name: "SvelteKit", link: "/installation/sveltekit" },
  { name: "Astro", link: "/installation/astro" },
  { name: "Angular", link: "/installation/angular" },
  { name: "Vite", link: "/installation/vite" },
  { name: "Eleventy", link: "/installation/eleventy" },
  { name: "Server-rendered", link: "/installation/cdn" }
] -%}

<main id="main-content" class="docs-home" data-pagefind-body>
	{#- The hero. Copy first in the document and on every screen: the
	    headline states the relationship, the demo below proves it. The
	    demo is a source pane and the card that source renders, side by
	    side from 48rem and stacked under it, never overlapping. The card
	    is rendered by the page's own Cirth, not a screenshot, and is
	    inert: it is a picture of the output, not a form on the home page. -#}
	<section class="docs-hero" aria-labelledby="home-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<div class="docs-hero-copy">
				<h1 id="home-title">Semantic HTML in.<br />Finished UI out.</h1>
				<p class="docs-hero-lede">{{ hero.tagline }}</p>
				<p class="docs-hero-actions">
					{% for action in hero.actions %}
						<a href="{{ action.link }}" role="button"{% if action.theme != "brand" %} class="secondary outline"{% endif %}>{{ action.text }}</a>
					{% endfor %}
				</p>
				<pre class="docs-hero-install" tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-sh">npm install @cirthcss/cirth</code></pre>
			</div>
			<figure class="docs-hero-demo">
				<div class="docs-hero-panes">
					<div class="docs-hero-pane docs-hero-source">
						<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><span>sign-in.html</span><span>HTML</span></p>
						<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ heroSpecimen | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
					</div>
					<p class="docs-hero-connector" aria-hidden="true"><span>renders</span></p>
					<div class="docs-hero-pane docs-hero-result">
						<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><span>Result</span><span>cirth.min.css</span></p>
						<div class="docs-hero-render" inert>{{ heroSpecimen | safe }}</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<figcaption id="hero-demo-caption" class="sr-only">A sign-in card: the HTML on one side and, on the other, the interface Cirth renders from it with no class added.</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<ul class="docs-hero-facts" aria-label="At a glance">
				<li><strong>0 B</strong> JavaScript runtime</li>
				<li><strong>{{ proof.size.label if proof.size else "About 15 KB" }}</strong> gzipped</li>
				<li><strong>WCAG 2.2 AA</strong> baseline</li>
				<li><strong>Any</strong> framework</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- The story, in three beats. Served complete: every beat carries its
	    own visual, so without script, on a phone, or with reduced motion,
	    this is three headed steps each followed by what it shows. The
	    script in this file upgrades it, on a wide screen with motion
	    allowed, to a sticky stage whose visual follows the heading in view.
	    Scrolling is never taken over: the page moves as it always does, and
	    only which visual is shown changes. -#}
	<section class="docs-story" aria-labelledby="story-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="story-title">Less markup. The same interface.</h2>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-story-steps" data-docs-story>
				{% for step in story %}
					<div class="docs-story-step" data-docs-story-step="{{ loop.index }}">
						<div class="docs-story-text">
							<h3>{{ step.title }}</h3>
							<p>{{ step.text }}</p>
						</div>
						<figure class="docs-story-visual">
							{% if loop.index == 1 %}
								<div class="docs-story-code">
									<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><span>invite.html</span><span>{{ storySoup | classCount }} class names</span></p>
									<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ storySoup | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
								</div>
								<figcaption id="story-visual-1">The card with a class vocabulary: {{ storySoup | classCount }} class names to write, read and keep in step.</figcaption>
							{% elif loop.index == 2 %}
								<div class="docs-story-code">
									<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><span>invite.html</span><span>{{ storySemantic | classCount }} class names</span></p>
									<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ storySemantic | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
								</div>
								<figcaption id="story-visual-2">The same card as semantic HTML: {{ storySemantic | classCount }} class names, and every element says what it is.</figcaption>
							{% else %}
								<div class="docs-story-render">
									<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><span>Result</span><span>cirth.min.css</span></p>
									<div class="docs-story-render-stage" inert>{{ storySemantic | safe }}</div>
								</div>
								<figcaption id="story-visual-3">What Cirth renders from it: a card with a title band, two labelled fields and a footer action.</figcaption>
							{% endif %}
						</figure>
					</div>
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- Framework agnostic. The diagram is the argument: four syntaxes, and
	    by the time the browser styles them they are one element. The
	    rendered button below it is inert for the same reason the hero's
	    card is. -#}
	<section class="docs-frameworks docs-band" aria-labelledby="frameworks-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="frameworks-title">Pure CSS. Framework agnostic. Works everywhere.</h2>
				<p>Cirth ships no React, Vue or Svelte components, and needs none. It styles the HTML that reaches the browser, so it makes no difference whether a template, a server or a client framework wrote it.</p>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-frameworks-layout">
				<figure class="docs-dom-diagram">
					<ul class="docs-syntax-list">
						{% for syntax in syntaxes %}
							<li><span>{{ syntax.name }}</span><code>{{ syntax.code }}</code></li>
						{% endfor %}
					</ul>
					<p class="docs-dom-arrow" aria-hidden="true"><span>one element in the DOM</span></p>
					<div class="docs-dom-result" inert>
						<code>&lt;button&gt;</code>
						<button type="button">Save</button>
					</div>
					<figcaption id="dom-diagram-caption">Four ways to write a button. Each one reaches the browser as the same <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, and Cirth styles that.</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<div class="docs-ecosystems">
					<h3>Guides for the tools you use</h3>
					<ul class="docs-ecosystem-list">
						{% for ecosystem in ecosystems %}
							<li><a href="{{ ecosystem.link }}">{{ ecosystem.name }}</a></li>
						{% endfor %}
					</ul>
					<p>Rails, Django, Laravel or a Go template: if it sends HTML, Cirth styles it. Every guide was checked in a real project with that tool's own starter.</p>
					<p class="docs-ecosystem-note">Names belong to their owners and appear only to say where Cirth has been checked. None of these projects is affiliated with Cirth or endorses it.</p>
					<p><a href="/installation/#framework-guides">All framework guides</a></p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- The showcase: three examples, one stage, and a strip above it. Every
	    panel is served rendered and unhidden; the tab strip ships `hidden`
	    and the script below turns it on, so without script this is three
	    complete examples stacked under their own headings. -#}
	<section class="docs-showcase" aria-labelledby="semantic-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="semantic-title">Start with the HTML you already know.</h2>
				<p>Standard elements carry the styling <em>and</em> the behaviour. Change the markup and you change the interface. Nothing in these three samples is wired to a script.</p>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-switch" data-docs-switch aria-label="Example" hidden>
				{% for example in examples %}
					<button type="button" id="tab-{{ example.id }}" data-docs-tab="{{ example.id }}">{{ example.tab }}</button>
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
			<div class="docs-stage">
				<header class="docs-stage-band">
					<span class="docs-stage-note">Try it with the keyboard</span>
					<span class="docs-stage-note"><code>cirth.min.css</code></span>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-stage-deck">
					{% for example in examples %}
						<div class="docs-example" id="panel-{{ example.id }}" data-docs-panel="{{ example.id }}">
							<h3 class="docs-example-name">{{ example.name }}</h3>
							<div class="docs-stage-body docs-stage-split">
								<div class="docs-stage-code">
									<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ example.markup | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
								</div>
								<div class="docs-stage-preview"><div>{{ example.markup | safe }}</div></div>
							</div>
							<p class="docs-example-note">{{ example.note | safe }}</p>
						</div>
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="docs-showcase-more"><a href="/examples">Browse complete examples</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- The theme demo, with its own copy of Cirth in a shadow root, so the
	    values it moves never touch the theme the reader picked for the
	    page. The listing is the stylesheet the element really carries. -#}
	<section class="docs-showcase docs-theme-showcase" aria-labelledby="theme-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="theme-title">Your theme. Same HTML.</h2>
				<p>Every colour, radius and font is a <code>--cirth-*</code> custom property. The markup on the right never changes and no class is added to it. The declarations on the left do, one at a time, and the finished interface follows them.</p>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-stage">
				<header class="docs-stage-band">
					<span class="docs-stage-note"><code>&lt;cirth-theme-preview&gt;</code> · its own Cirth, in a shadow root</span>
					<button type="button" class="docs-stage-toggle" data-docs-theme-toggle hidden>Pause</button>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-stage-body docs-stage-split docs-stage-split-narrow">
					<div class="docs-stage-code">
						<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-css" data-docs-theme-block>{{ themePreview.block | safe }}</code></pre>
						<ul class="docs-token-legend" data-docs-theme-legend>
							{% for token in themePreview.tokens %}
								<li data-token="{{ token }}"><span class="docs-token-chip" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>{{ token }}</code></li>
							{% endfor %}
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="docs-stage-preview">
						<cirth-theme-preview data-docs-theme-preview>{{ themeSpecimen | safe }}</cirth-theme-preview>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="docs-example-note">Real values, from the files that declare them: the default theme in <code>cirth.scoped.css</code>, and the <code>plain</code> and <code>playroom</code> presets compiled from <code>src/presets/</code>. In your own page the same declarations go on <code>:root</code>. Check <a href="/customization">every token, and how to override it</a>.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- The proof, one claim per card. Each says what kind of statement it
	    is and where to check it, and the check path is a full-width link
	    at the foot of its card rather than a word at the end of a line. -#}
	<section class="docs-proof docs-band" aria-labelledby="proof-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="proof-title">Every claim has a check path.</h2>
				<p>{{ pitch.lede }}</p>
			</header>
			<ul class="docs-claim-list">
				{% for claim in claims %}
					<li class="docs-claim">
						{{ proofKind(claim.kind) }}
						<h3>{{ claim.title }}</h3>
						<p>{{ claim.text | safe }}</p>
						<a href="{{ claim.link }}">{{ claim.check }}<span class="sr-only">: {{ claim.title }}</span></a>
					</li>
				{% endfor %}
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	{% if faq %}
		<section class="docs-native-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
			<div class="docs-home-inner">
				<header class="docs-section-head">
					<h2 id="faq-title">{{ faq.title }}</h2>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-faq-list">
					{% set measuredSize = proof.size.label if proof.size else "about 15 KB" %}
					{% for item in faq.items %}
						{{ faqDisclosure(item.q, item.a | replace("<!--browsers-->", browsers.sentence) | replace("<!--size-->", measuredSize)) }}
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	{% endif %}

	<section class="docs-cta" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<h2 id="cta-title">Add one stylesheet. Keep your HTML.</h2>
			<p>Start from a CDN link or an npm import. The framework guides cover the rest.</p>
			{{ packageManagers("home") }}
			<p class="docs-hero-actions">
				<a href="/installation" role="button">Read the installation guide</a>
				<a href="/why-cirth" role="button" class="secondary outline">Why Cirth</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</main>


<script>
	// The showcase's tab strip. Everything it needs is already in the page:
	// three rendered examples, each with its own heading, served visible.
	// This turns them into one tab panel at a time and turns the strip on,
	// so a reader without script gets three complete examples rather than a
	// row of buttons that do nothing, and the ARIA roles only ever exist
	// while there is something implementing them.
	(() => {
		const strip = document.querySelector("[data-docs-switch]");
		if (!strip) return;
		const tabs = [...strip.querySelectorAll("[data-docs-tab]")];
		const panels = tabs.map((tab) =>
			document.querySelector(`[data-docs-panel="${tab.dataset.docsTab}"]`),
		);
		if (!tabs.length || panels.some((panel) => !panel)) return;

		// Roles from here rather than from the template: without this script
		// the strip is hidden and the panels are just three headed sections,
		// and a `role="tabpanel"` with no tablist anywhere would be a lie
		// about the document.
		strip.setAttribute("role", "tablist");
		for (const [index, tab] of tabs.entries()) {
			const panel = /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (panels[index]);
			tab.setAttribute("role", "tab");
			tab.setAttribute("aria-controls", panel.id);
			panel.setAttribute("role", "tabpanel");
			panel.setAttribute("aria-labelledby", tab.id);
			// Reachable, because a panel holding a scrollable <pre> and a form
			// is content a reader arrives at from the tab.
			panel.setAttribute("tabindex", "0");
		}

		/** @param {number} next @param {boolean} moveFocus */
		const select = (next, moveFocus) => {
			for (const [index, tab] of tabs.entries()) {
				const current = index === next;
				tab.setAttribute("aria-selected", String(current));
				// Roving tabindex: one stop for the whole strip, and the arrow
				// keys walk it (WAI-ARIA tabs pattern).
				tab.tabIndex = current ? 0 : -1;
				/** @type {HTMLElement} */ (panels[index]).hidden = !current;
			}
			if (moveFocus) /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (tabs[next]).focus();
		};

		strip.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
			const tab = /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (event.target)?.closest?.(
				"[data-docs-tab]",
			);
			if (tab) select(tabs.indexOf(/** @type {HTMLElement} */ (tab)), false);
		});

		// Selection follows focus, which is the pattern's default where every
		// panel is already in the document: nothing is fetched or built by
		// arrowing across the strip. There is no autoplay to interrupt: a
		// panel that changed itself would take a <details> the reader had
		// just opened, or a field they were typing in, out from under them,
		// and no amount of pausing makes that worth having.
		strip.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
			const from = tabs.findIndex((tab) => tab === document.activeElement);
			if (from < 0) return;
			const step = { ArrowRight: 1, ArrowDown: 1, ArrowLeft: -1, ArrowUp: -1 }[
				event.key
			];
			const to = step
				? (from + step + tabs.length) % tabs.length
				: event.key === "Home"
					? 0
					: event.key === "End"
						? tabs.length - 1
						: -1;
			if (to < 0) return;
			event.preventDefault();
			select(to, true);
		});

		strip.hidden = false;
		select(0, false);
	})();

	// <cirth-theme-preview>: the theme demo, with its own copy of Cirth.
	//
	// A documentation element, not a Cirth API: nothing in the published
	// package knows about it, and the section it serves is the only place it
	// exists. What it buys is isolation, which this particular demo cannot
	// work without. The section changes --cirth-* values live; the page
	// around it has to keep the theme the reader picked in the header; and
	// the two have to be visible at the same time for the claim to land.
	// Done in the page's own cascade that is a set of overrides fighting the
	// framework for specificity, and every one of them a place where the
	// demo and the documentation can disagree.
	//
	// A shadow root removes the fight instead of winning it:
	//
	//   · the page's element rules (article, button, input …) do not cross
	//     the boundary, so nothing in here is styled by the site's build;
	//   · custom properties do inherit in, and the scoped build re-declares
	//     every one of them on `.cirth`, so the page's values are shadowed
	//     for this subtree by the demo's own stylesheet rather than by a
	//     more specific selector;
	//   · the theme is one ordinary stylesheet after Cirth's, overriding
	//     custom properties at the same specificity, which is exactly what
	//     the Customization page tells an author to write.
	//
	// The stylesheet it loads is the real compiled scoped build (the same
	// artifact the /lab/ specimens use, compiled from src/ with the public
	// scoped switch), and the markup is the element's own children, served
	// by the template and cloned in. Without script those children are what
	// a reader gets: the page's Cirth renders the same specimen in the light
	// DOM.
	const ThemePreview = class extends HTMLElement {
		/** @type {HTMLStyleElement | null} */
		#theme = null;

		connectedCallback() {
			if (this.shadowRoot) return;
			const root = this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
			const base = (document.documentElement.dataset.base || "/").replace(
				/\/$/,
				"",
			);

			const cirth = document.createElement("link");
			cirth.rel = "stylesheet";
			cirth.href = `${base}/styles/generated/cirth-lab-scoped.css`;

			// The element's own layout: the part the docs shell owns. The
			// padding lives in here rather than on the pane outside, so the
			// surface --cirth-canvas paints is the whole half of the stage:
			// when the demo's canvas moves, the page's canvas is right beside
			// it across a hairline, which is the isolation being visible
			// rather than described.
			const shell = document.createElement("style");
			shell.textContent = `
				:host { display: grid; }
				.cirth {
					display: grid;
					align-content: center;
					padding: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
				}
				.cirth > :first-child { margin-block-start: 0; }
				.cirth > :last-child { margin-block-end: 0; }

				/* The values change on their own, so the surfaces they paint
				   move rather than cut. Subtractive, as everywhere else on
				   this page: declared only under no-preference, and the
				   sequence is correct without it. */
				@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
					.cirth, .cirth * {
						transition:
							background-color 0.3s ease,
							border-color 0.3s ease,
							color 0.3s ease,
							border-radius 0.3s ease;
					}
				}
			`;

			// After Cirth's, and empty until the section writes to it.
			const theme = document.createElement("style");
			theme.dataset.cirthTheme = "";

			// The fallback, moved rather than copied. Left behind it would be a
			// second, unrendered copy of a form: invisible, unfocusable, and
			// still matching every selector anything on this page looks with.
			const surface = document.createElement("div");
			surface.className = "cirth";
			surface.append(...this.children);

			root.append(cirth, shell, theme, surface);
			this.#theme = theme;

			// One thing a stylesheet cannot say: a shadow tree is styled by
			// its own sheet, so the scheme the page is in has to be handed to
			// it. The scoped build reads `data-theme` off the `.cirth`
			// element itself, which is what this sets: the demo isolates the
			// theme's tokens, not whether the site is in the dark.
			const scheme = () => {
				surface.dataset.theme =
					document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark"
						? "dark"
						: "light";
			};
			scheme();
			new MutationObserver(scheme).observe(document.documentElement, {
				attributeFilter: ["data-theme"],
			});
		}

		/**
		 * The demo's stylesheet, replaced whole. The caller passes the same
		 * text the listing beside the preview is showing.
		 * @param {string} css
		 */
		setTheme(css) {
			if (this.#theme) this.#theme.textContent = css;
		}
	};

	if (!customElements.get("cirth-theme-preview")) {
		customElements.define("cirth-theme-preview", ThemePreview);
	}

	// The declarations, one at a time.
	//
	// Every value here is a real one, read out of a compiled file by
	// themePreview() in docs/eleventy.config.js: the default theme as the
	// scoped build declares it, and each preset as its own stylesheet
	// declares it. The listing and the element are written from the same
	// string in the same step, so the code on the left cannot be describing
	// something the interface on the right is not doing.
	//
	// Deliberately not a type-out. The claim is "the values moved and the
	// markup did not", and a terminal pretending to be typed says the
	// opposite: that the stylesheet is a performance. A line is marked, its
	// value is replaced, the element takes it, and the interface settles.
	(() => {
		const preview = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-preview]");
		const block = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-block]");
		const legend = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-legend]");
		const toggle = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-toggle]");
		if (!(preview instanceof ThemePreview)) return;
		if (!(block instanceof HTMLElement) || !(toggle instanceof HTMLElement)) {
			return;
		}

		const selector = {{ themePreview.selector | dump | safe }};
		const tokens = {{ themePreview.tokens | dump | safe }};
		const states = {{ themePreview.states | dump | safe }};

		// What the element is carrying. It starts as the block the page was
		// served with, which is the first state, so nothing moves on load.
		const current = new Map(
			tokens.map((token) => [token, states[0].lines[token]]),
		);

		const write = () => {
			const declarations = tokens
				.map((token) => `  ${token}: ${current.get(token).value};`)
				.join("\n");
			preview.setTheme(`${selector} {\n${declarations}\n}`);
			// The chips take the same declarations. A swatch is a claim about
			// a value, so it has to be painted by the value the preview has;
			// left to the page's own tokens it would show the site's accent
			// beside a preview carrying a different one.
			if (legend) {
				legend.setAttribute(
					"style",
					tokens
						.map((token) => `${token}: ${current.get(token).value}`)
						.join("; "),
				);
			}
		};
		write();

		// The sequence: every state in turn, one token per step, and only
		// where the value actually moves; a step that changes nothing would
		// mark a line to say it had changed. It ends on the state it started
		// from, so the loop closes without a jump.
		const steps = [];
		const running = new Map(current);
		for (let round = 1; round <= states.length; round += 1) {
			for (const token of tokens) {
				const line = states[round % states.length].lines[token];
				if (running.get(token).value === line.value) continue;
				running.set(token, line);
				steps.push({ token, line });
			}
		}

		const LEAD = 380;
		const DWELL = 1600;
		let at = 0;
		let timer = 0;
		let generation = 0;
		let playing = false;
		let onscreen = false;

		const pause = () => {
			generation += 1;
			clearTimeout(timer);
			timer = 0;
		};

		/** @param {string} token */
		const mark = (token) => {
			for (const line of block.querySelectorAll(".docs-token")) {
				line.classList.remove("is-changed");
			}
			const line = block.querySelector(`.docs-token[data-token="${token}"]`);
			if (!(line instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
			// Re-added after a forced reflow, because the same animation on
			// the same element does not restart on its own.
			void line.offsetWidth;
			line.classList.add("is-changed");
		};

		const advance = () => {
			const mine = generation;
			const step = steps[at];
			at = (at + 1) % steps.length;
			mark(step.token);
			timer = setTimeout(() => {
				if (mine !== generation) return;
				const line = block.querySelector(
					`.docs-token[data-token="${step.token}"]`,
				);
				if (line) line.innerHTML = step.line.html;
				current.set(step.token, step.line);
				write();
				timer = setTimeout(advance, DWELL);
			}, LEAD);
		};

		const play = () => {
			if (timer || !playing || !onscreen || steps.length === 0) return;
			timer = setTimeout(advance, DWELL);
		};

		/** @param {boolean} next */
		const setPlaying = (next) => {
			playing = next;
			toggle.textContent = next ? "Pause" : "Play";
			toggle.setAttribute(
				"aria-label",
				next ? "Pause the theme demo" : "Play the theme demo",
			);
			if (next) play();
			else pause();
		};

		toggle.hidden = false;
		toggle.addEventListener("click", () => setPlaying(!playing));

		// Nothing runs while the section is off screen: the demo exists to be
		// watched, and a page that has never been scrolled this far has
		// nothing to watch it.
		new IntersectionObserver(
			(entries) => {
				onscreen = entries.some((entry) => entry.isIntersecting);
				if (onscreen) play();
				else pause();
			},
			{ threshold: 0.25 },
		).observe(preview);

		// Autoplay only where motion is welcome. With the preference set the
		// section is served in its first state and holds there, and the
		// control is still live, so the sequence is available on request,
		// with the value changes landing immediately because the transitions
		// are declared under no-preference only.
		const calm = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
		setPlaying(!calm.matches);
		calm.addEventListener("change", () => {
			if (calm.matches) setPlaying(false);
		});
	})();
</script>
